## ❌ ANTI-PATTERNS (FORBIDDEN)

| Anti-Pattern | Why Wrong | Correct Approach |
|--------------|-----------|------------------|
| Auto-generate complete agent | User loses control over design | Conversational: goal → architecture → RISEN → phases → generate |
| Skip RISEN design | Agent identity undefined | Design element-by-element with user |
| Skip phase design | Workflow undefined | Design phase-by-phase with user |
| **Default target-agent phase count to 8 (Agent Builder anchoring bias)** | Agent Builder's own 8-phase workflow is referenced ~10 times across its files; this anchors every proposed target-agent count to 8 regardless of complexity. Causes bloated Simple/Medium agents and under-thought Complex ones. | Derive phase count from Complexity Tier (Simple 4-5 / Medium 5-7 / Complex 7-10). Show tier + rationale + an alternative sizing in Phase 1 Architecture Proposal. Lock total in Phase 2B.i BEFORE per-phase design. |
| Add phases incrementally in Phase 2B per-phase iteration | Phase count drifts upward because "one more phase" feels low-cost in the moment but compounds. | Lock total count in 2B.i first; in 2B.ii only allow merge/split with explicit re-lock, never silent addition. |
| Treat Phase 2 "add" button as an unbounded append | Same as above — incremental drift. | Every "add" in 2B.ii must update the locked count and re-confirm it's still within Complexity Tier range. |
| Missing prompt techniques | Architecture non-compliant | Embed all 14 techniques |
| Hardcode section numbers in rules | Breaks decoupling | Section numbers → templates/ only |
| Dump entire file in chat (Phase 3-5) | User can't review properly, floods conversation | Write file to output directory, show 3-5 line summary + file path in chat |
| Show file content in code block instead of writing file | Same as above — anti-pattern | Use IDE file writing tools, not chat output |
| Emit Mermaid `flowchart`/`graph` without the `curve: 'linear'` init directive | Default curved bezier edges overlap in dense diagrams; GitHub/VS Code/Cursor/Windsurf renderers all default to curved if no directive | Start every flowchart with `%%{ init: { 'flowchart': { 'curve': 'linear' } } }%%` per `@lib/docs/mermaid-diagram-best-practices.md` §3 |
| Use `\n` for line breaks in Mermaid node/edge labels | `\n` renders as literal `\n` in GitHub, VS Code preview, Cursor, Windsurf, and most Mermaid Live configs — visible bug that ships to production | Always use `<br/>` per `@lib/docs/mermaid-diagram-best-practices.md` §2 Rule #1 |
| Interleave `style` declarations with node/connection statements | Hard to maintain; easy to duplicate or miss one; obscures logical flow | Put ALL `style` lines at the end of the diagram in node-declaration order per `@lib/docs/mermaid-diagram-best-practices.md` §5.4 |
| Emit a generated agent without embedding Mermaid Standards in its Guidance + Stop Points | Newly generated agent has no guardrails against the three mistakes above — ships broken diagrams from day one | Every agent whose output includes Mermaid diagrams gets the §12.2 embeddable template in Guidance + the §12.4 MERMAID RENDERING GUARD in Stop Points |
| Mutate `.mdc`/`.md`/`.yaml`/`.json` via `sed`/`awk`/`cat >>`/`echo >>` | Platform drift (BSD vs GNU), silent partial success, no pre-read, no diff preview — Gap #15 Tool Misuse Hazard | Use `read_file` + `edit`/`multi_edit`/`write_to_file` — native IDE tools are atomic, exact-match, and show diffs |
| Single edit / write larger than ~300 lines (long-form deliverables) | Gap #16 IDE Edit-Size Failure Mode: truncation, silent mid-section drift, structural invariant breakage, error cascade, positional bias amplification | Progressive Documentation Pattern (Technique #20): skeleton-first, then per-phase bounded appends with targeted `edit`/`multi_edit` (≤300 lines each) |
| Regenerate full file to modify one section | Risks full-file truncation, loses validated earlier content, triggers `old_string not unique` / `no match` errors | Use targeted `edit` with narrow `old_string` matching only the affected region; pre-read before every edit |
| **Wait-Until-Final-Phase generation in a multi-phase agent (v1.5.0 anti-pattern)** | Even short-form deliverables (<300 lines) fail when all content is held in chat until a single final write — context window exhausts mid-run, the final write loses earlier-phase decisions, positional bias drops middle sections. Root cause of CSA v1.6.0 healthcare-recommendations failure. | Pattern #20 v2 Per-Phase Write Contract: every phase writes its approved content to the corresponding section marker BEFORE its checkpoint; final phase is Consolidate-and-Polish (not from-scratch). Set `progressiveDocV2: true` in Phase 0. |
| **Final phase regenerates earlier-approved prose (v1.5.0 anti-pattern)** | Violates Mechanic 4 of Pattern #20 v2; risks regression on previously approved content; wastes context window on already-finalized sections | The final phase is CONSOLIDATE mode only — re-read file, apply cross-section consistency edits, replace residual placeholders, run style/length self-check. Do NOT regenerate sections already written and approved in earlier phases. |
| **Generated agent has hard line cap (e.g., "≤250 lines") instead of example-anchored conciseness (v1.5.0 anti-pattern)** | Hard caps create the wrong incentive: agents truncate substance to hit a number. Conciseness should be benchmarked against canonical example output, not against a count. | Replace any "must be ≤ N lines" rule in the generated agent's Guidance/Stop Points with "benchmark length against `{exampleAnchorPath}` (which lands at ~{X} lines for this complexity tier)". Add structural budgets (sentence counts, table density, K-word quote rules) instead. See `@01_Guidance.mdc` §10.5. |
| **Generated agent lacks Phase 0 skeleton step when `progressiveDocV2: true` (v1.5.0 anti-pattern)** | Without skeleton creation, later phases have nothing to `edit` into — they fall back to chat accumulation, defeating Pattern #20 v2 entirely | Generated agent's Phase 0 task list MUST include "Create deliverable skeleton with named placeholders" as Step 1 when `progressiveDocV2: true`. Placeholders MUST be unique and greppable (e.g., `<!-- SECTION: section-name -->`). |
| Skip Scripts Pillar or State Persistence Assessment in Phase 0 | User loses control over scripts/state design decisions | Both assessments MUST run in Phase 0 with explicit user confirmation |
| Include State File sections when `stateful: false` | Violates conditional skeleton contract; bloats stateless agents | Skip State File, State Update Protocol, Resume Capability entirely; use optional `run_metadata.json` audit snapshot instead |
| Generate 20+ phases | Over-engineered | 4-7 meaningful phases |
| Stop at every micro-step | Annoying, slows workflow | Stop at key decision junctures |

